## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses practical enterprise RAG architecture with multi-tenant isolation, policy governance, and regulatory compliance across global regions. It tests understanding of data locality requirements, policy lifecycle management, and comprehensive testing strategies—critical components for production LLM deployments serving Fortune 500 clients.

## Key Concepts
- Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) with tenant isolation
- Region-aware routing and data locality enforcement
- Policy versioning with TTLs and automated eviction
- Vector store segmentation and encryption
- Compliance testing (GDPR/CCPA) and drift detection

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: enforce tenant-scoped lookups and region routing
function routeQuery(req) {
  const { tenantId, region } = req;
  const policyVersion = getCurrentPolicyVersion(tenantId);
  const vectorStore = getTenantVectorStore(`${tenantId}_${region}`);

  if (!isCompliantWithPolicy(req, policyVersion)) {
    throw new PolicyViolationError();
  }

  return vectorStore.search(req.query, {
    tenantId,
    policyVersion,
    dataMinimization: true
  });
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question assesses the ability to design data lineage and prompt hygiene features in a practical, beginner-friendly manner. It evaluates understanding of per-tenant privacy requirements and the ability to translate abstract privacy needs into concrete models and tests without requiring knowledge of large-scale system design.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant provenance tracking (input, output, model version)
- Simple redaction policy for PII detection and handling during ingestion and storage
- Lightweight policy schema and data model design
- Minimal test plan with concrete PII examples
- Data residency considerations and auditability

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal data lineage model
type LineageRecord = {
  tenantId: string;
  requestId: string;
  timestamp: Date;
  input: {
    prompt: string;
    redactedPrompt?: string;
    tokens: number;
  };
  output?: {
    response: string;
    redactedResponse?: string;
    tokens: number;
  };
  model: {
    provider: string;
    version: string;
  };
};

// Simple redaction policy
const redactionPolicy = {
  patterns: [
    { type: 'EMAIL', regex: /\b[\w.-]+@[\w.-]+\.\w+\b/g },
    { type: 'PHONE', regex: /\b\d{3}[-.]?\d{3}[-.]?\d{4}\b/g },
    { type: 'SSN', regex: /\b\d{3}-\d{2}-\d{4}\b/g },
  ],
  replacement: '[REDACTED]',
};

function redactPII(text: string): string {
  let redacted = text;
  for (const { regex } of redactionPolicy.patterns) {
    redacted = redacted.replace(regex, redactionPolicy.replacement);
  }
  return redacted;
}
```

